            <ArticleTitle>Steroid celiac plexus block for chronic pancreatitis: results in 16 cases.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>431-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Sixteen cases in which celiac plexus block with depot steroid was used to treat chronic pancreatitis pain were reviewed. Only 4 of 16 patients reported pain relief with the procedure. Of the 12 patients who did not obtain relief, narcotic dependence was present in 11 of 12. No patients in the &quot;relief&quot; group were narcotic dependent. Prior pancreatic surgery was present in 9 of the 12 patients without relief and in 1 of 4 patients with relief. It is postulated that refractory chronic pancreatitis pain may be an extreme form of what has been termed &quot;abnormal illness behavior.&quot; Furthermore, these results underscore the poor results experienced using neural blockade for the relief of chronic pain when narcotic dependence is present.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
